 "answer": "The recent direction is toward **realistic, multi-step tool use and end-to-end workflows**, rather than just isolated tool calls. MCP-Bench and MLR-Bench illustrate that shift. [W3, W4]\n\n- **Earlier foundation—AgentBench:** The indexed paper evaluates 29 LLMs across eight interactive environments, including operating systems, databases, knowledge graphs, games, household tasks, web shopping, and browsing. It reported a substantial gap between leading commercial APIs and many open-source models of 70B parameters or fewer—a result from that evaluation, not a current leaderboard claim. [C2, C4]\n- **Current web information, retrieved October 27, 2026—MCP-Bench:** Listed as an **ICLR 2026 poster**, it tests agents through **28 live Model Context Protocol servers spanning 250 tools**. Tasks require tool discovery, cross-tool coordination, precise parameters, and multi-step planning; its evaluation of 20 advanced LLMs reports persistent challenges. [W3]\n- **Broader workflow evaluation—MLR-Bench:** Listed at **NeurIPS 2025**, it includes **201 open-ended machine-learning research tasks**, with assessments spanning idea generation, proposal formulation, experimentation, and paper writing—not merely whether an individual tool call succeeds. [W2, W4]\n\n**Yes—ReAct did end up at a major conference: ICLR 2023.** The paper itself states “Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2023,” and Princeton’s publication record independently confirms its presentation there. These publication-status sources were retrieved October 27, 2026. [W5, W6]",
